#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119436 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a9c2fbd5-33cf-51fb-b95d-cf5277f3e4ae" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Tangent Vector"}] :question/id #uuid "7d93dd10-5319-58e7-9106-f8c675dc9e65" :question/math-academy-id "e-12567" :question/problem "Find a tangent vector to the curve $\\mathbf r(t)=t\\,\\mathbf i+t^2\\,\\mathbf j+t^3\\,\\mathbf k$ at the point $P(1,1,1)$." :question/worked-solution "The point $P(1,1,1)$ is at the tip of $\\mathbf r(t)$ for some specific value $t_0$ of the parameter $t$. At this point, the vector $\\mathbf r'(t_0)$ is tangent to the curve.\n\nFirst, we find the value of $t$ that corresponds to $P$. We know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr(t) &= i + j + k \\\\\nti + t^{2}j + t^{3}k &= i + j + k\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand equating the coefficients of $\\mathbf i, \\mathbf j$, and $\\mathbf k$ yields the following system:\n\n$$\n{t = 1; t^{2} = 1; t^{3} = 1\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we get $t=1$. This value also satisfies the other two equations. Therefore, $P(1,1,1)$ is at the tip of the vector $\\mathbf r(1)$.\n\nNow, we calculate $\\mathbf r'(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=1$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{′}(t) &= \\frac{d}{dt}(t)i + \\frac{d}{dt}(t^{2})j + \\frac{d}{dt}(t^{3})k \\\\\n&= i + 2tj + 3t^{2}k \\\\\nr^{′}(1) &= i + 2(1)j + 3(1)^{2}k \\\\\n&= i + 2j + 3k\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a tangent vector to the given curve at at the point $P(1,1,1)$ is $\\,\\mathbf i+2\\,\\mathbf j+3\\,\\mathbf k$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1792/Source/Images/1d982638f3bd912bc8329318369e03d5.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119437 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "60e28b26-0eb7-51ed-bcf9-2660b98a6028" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Tangent Line to a Curve"}] :question/id #uuid "42109de0-eb59-53f9-982e-1433f630b067" :question/math-academy-id "e-12568" :question/problem "Find a parametrization of the tangent line to the curve $\\mathbf r(t)=\\left\\langle \\cos{t}, \\: \\sin{t},\\: 4t^2\\right\\rangle$ at the point where $t=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$." :question/worked-solution "Let $P$ be the point at the tip of the vector\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right)\n$$\n\nAt this point, the vector\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r'\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right)\n$$\n\nis tangent to the curve, so the tangent line to the curve at $P$ can be parametrized as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf R(u)=\\mathbf r\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right)+u \\, \\mathbf r'\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right), \\qquad u \\in (-\\infty, \\infty)\n$$\n\nFirst, we find\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right)\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr(\\frac{π}{2}) &= ⟨\\cos (\\frac{π}{2}), \\sin (\\frac{π}{2}), 4(\\frac{π}{2})^{2}⟩ = ⟨0, 1, π^{2}⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate $\\mathbf r'(t)$ and evaluate it at\n\n$$\nt=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{′}(t) &= ⟨\\frac{d}{dt}(\\cos t), \\frac{d}{dt}(\\sin t), \\frac{d}{dt}(4t^{2})⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨ - \\sin t, \\cos t, 8t⟩ \\\\\nr^{′}(\\frac{π}{2}) &= ⟨ - \\sin (\\frac{π}{2}), \\cos (\\frac{π}{2}), 8(\\frac{π}{2})⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨ - 1, 0, 4π⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the given curve at the point $P$ where\n\n$$\nt=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n$$\nR(u) = ⟨0, 1, π^{2}⟩ + u⟨ - 1, 0, 4π⟩, u ∈ (- ∞, ∞)\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1792/Source/Images/44a11a13457036bfe5aed9e5772179be.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119438 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b4636870-89db-5bf9-8a04-777407957e33" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Points Where a Tangent Vector is Parallel to Another Vector"}] :question/id #uuid "26c3579b-5b24-5e84-9024-5b2597856d20" :question/math-academy-id "e-12569" :question/problem "Given the curve $\\mathbf r(t)=(t^2+t)\\,\\mathbf i+(t^2-t)\\,\\mathbf j$, find the point at which the tangent to the curve is parallel to the vector $3\\, \\mathbf i+\\,\\mathbf j$." :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the derivative of $\\mathbf r(t)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{′}(t) &= \\frac{d}{dt}(t^{2} + t)i + \\frac{d}{dt}(t^{2} - t)j \\\\\n&= (2t + 1)i + (2t - 1)j\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors $\\mathbf r'(t)$ and $3\\, \\mathbf i+\\,\\mathbf j$ are parallel for some value of the parameter $t$ if and only if there exists a constant $c$ such that\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r'(t)=c(3\\, \\mathbf i+\\,\\mathbf j)\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n(2t + 1)i + (2t - 1)j = c(3i + j)\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\mathbf i$ and $\\mathbf j$ gives the following system:\n\n$$\n{2t + 1 = 3c; 2t - 1 = c\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get $c=2t-1$. Substituting into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2t + 1 &= 3c \\\\\n2t + 1 &= 3(2t - 1) \\\\\n2t + 1 &= 6t - 3 \\\\\n6t - 2t &= 1 + 3 \\\\\n4t &= 4 \\\\\nt &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\mathbf r'$ and $3\\, \\mathbf i+\\,\\mathbf j$ are parallel only at the point corresponding to $t=1$.\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr(1) &= (1^{2} + 1)i + (1^{2} - 1)j = 2i\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe conclude that $\\mathbf r'(t)$ and $3\\, \\mathbf i+\\,\\mathbf j$ are parallel at the point $(2,0)$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1792/Source/Images/f28e49053aee4f84b3dff0ab2a0ec87d.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119439 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "297a4812-a87a-592a-818a-8c8d3d3f7868" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Points Where a Tangent Vector is Perpendicular to Another Vector"}] :question/id #uuid "e8c24f26-7658-5251-922a-4b34043a71d4" :question/math-academy-id "e-12580" :question/problem "Given the curve $\\mathbf r(t)=\\left\\langle t^2+1\\,,\\, 6t+3\\right\\rangle$, find all of the points at which the tangent to the curve is perpendicular to the vector $\\left\\langle 3\\,,\\, 1\\right\\rangle$." :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the derivative of $\\mathbf r(t)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{′}(t) &= ⟨\\frac{d}{dt}(t^{2} + 1), \\frac{d}{dt}(6t + 3)⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨2t, 6⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors\n\n$$\n\\left\\langle 3\\,,\\, 1 \\right\\rangle\n$$\n\nand $\\mathbf r'(t)$ are perpendicular for some value of the parameter $t$ if and only if\n\n$$\n\\left\\langle 3\\,,\\, 1 \\right\\rangle \\cdot \\mathbf r'(t) = 0\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n⟨3, 1⟩ \\cdot r^{′}(t) &= 0 \\\\\n⟨3, 1⟩ \\cdot ⟨2t, 6⟩ &= 0 \\\\\n3 \\cdot 2t + 1 \\cdot 6 &= 0 \\\\\n6t + 6 &= 0 \\\\\nt &= -1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the only solution is $t=-1$.\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr(-1) &= ⟨(-1)^{2} + 1, 6(-1) + 3⟩ = ⟨2,-3⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe conclude that\n\n$$\n\\left\\langle 3\\,,\\, 1\\right\\rangle\n$$\n\nand $\\mathbf r'(t)$ are perpendicular at the point $(2,-3)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120145 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a9c2fbd5-33cf-51fb-b95d-cf5277f3e4ae" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Tangent Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c43b6daf-aa85-5bba-98c1-47b6a22b594a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨ - 2, 2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117391} {:answer/id #uuid "d7d88588-ef76-5b00-bb1d-b137c461ad7d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨4, 4⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117392} {:answer/id #uuid "de9a1398-aed1-5a44-8ab3-a2e31fb96c6b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨2, 2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117393} {:answer/id #uuid "0b5b6cab-7226-55ef-bdaf-b517cc71e5c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨4, 2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117394} {:answer/id #uuid "6c0ab2de-0954-547c-930d-34fd15fd5a29" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨4,-2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117395}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0b5b6cab-7226-55ef-bdaf-b517cc71e5c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨4, 2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117394} :answer-field/id #uuid "fa839e10-db01-577e-91c1